course: dictionary
lesson: 6860
title: Overboard: over the side, or too far
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Overboard is an adverb. It tells you where something went: over the side of a boat or ship and into the water. «In a storm Jonah was thrown overboard.» The clue is that he was thrown from the ship, so he ended up in the sea.
explain: Context helps again. «They hove the wine-casks overboard» tells you the barrels went over the side of the boat. Try your meaning in the sentence: "thrown over the side into the water" still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Overboard also has an informal meaning. To go overboard means to do too much of something, to be too excited or too extreme. «Don't go overboard with the decorations» means don't overdo it. The clues are the word "don't" and the idea of doing too much.
fact: Overboard means over the side of a boat or ship and into the water.
fact: «Man overboard!» is shouted when someone falls off a ship.
fact: Life jackets help you float if you fall overboard.
fact: To go overboard means to do too much, for example with decorations or spending.
exercise: «In a storm Jonah was thrown overboard.» What does overboard mean here? | Inside the cabin, out of the rain | *Over the side of the ship and into the water | Up into the sky | Under the deck of the ship
exercise: Mum says, «Don't go overboard with the decorations.» What does she mean? | Hang them over the side of the boat | *Don't overdo it, keep them simple | Put up as many as you can | Throw the decorations into the sea
exercise: Find and copy the word that means threw in «They hove the wine-casks overboard». | casks | wine | overboard | *hove
test: «Man overboard!» is shouted when what happens? | A boat is painted bright blue | A ship sails into the harbour | Someone sings a song on deck | *Someone falls off a ship into the sea
test: What is the opposite of overboard, for a person on a ship? | Overhead | *On board | Far away | Under the sea
exam: Which sentence uses overboard in the informal sense? | *She bought far too much party food, more than anyone could eat, because she went overboard. | Her hat blew over the side of the boat. | She fell off the ferry and landed in the sea. | The crew threw the casks overboard.
